  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 629 PM EDT SAT JUL 25 2026\n\nAn amplified and pretty stagnant long wave pattern will remain in\nplace over the CONUS through next week. The center of an \nanomalously strong upper level ridge will gradually shift west\nover the Four Corners region, while troughing dominates east of\nthe Mississippi River. This will allow for northwesterly flow to\ncontinue to be in place aloft across the Ohio and Tennessee\nvalleys early in the upcoming week, with unsettled weather\nexpected. As troughing sharpens up more so across the Eastern \nSeaboard by the middle and latter half of the work week, enough \nresidual 500 mb height rises will occur across eastern Kentucky to\nallow for a return of drier weather from Wednesday through \nFriday, before at least small rain chances return by Saturday, as\nupper level height falls return across the region.\n\nThe threat of MCS activity will be on the table from Monday\nthrough Tuesday across eastern Kentucky. Model guidance has been\ninconsistent in positioning and timing, typical even within 24\nhours or less, let alone 36-48 hours out. At this time, the\nmajority of model guidance is pointing more towards a quieter \ndaytime period on Monday, as Sunday night's activity looks to stay\nand exit mainly to our west. Heat and humidity will continue to \nbuild in across the area, with highs in the upper 80s to around 90\nand dew points in the lower 70s. While most activity currently \nlooks to move in after sunset, given the potential for upstream \noutflow migration and influence, having at least 20-30 PoPs in \nplace as the latest guidance has offered up seems reasonable. \nBetter PoPs move in Monday night and linger into Tuesday. The \nprogressive nature of this activity lends to less of a hydro \nconcern; however, freezing levels will remain high and PWATs will \nbe peaking in the 75th-90th percentile range, so some quick bursts\nof heavy rainfall are still possible. Additionally, any residual \noutflow boundaries aligning more parallel with the upper level \nflow will also need to be monitored, as this would be more \nconducive to training cells. Cold pool intensity and longevity\nwill dictate the strong to damaging wind gust potential, and this\nthreat will exist Monday and Monday night across our area.\n\nAnother hot and humid day is on tap for Tuesday, with additional \nMCS activity potentially in the vicinity, although even more \nuncertainty exists regarding the details. A surface cold front \nwill usher in a cooler and drier air mass for the area by \nWednesday, with surface high pressure settling across the Ohio \nValley. Highs will retreat to more seasonable lower to middle 80s,\nwhile lows range from the low to mid 60s at night. Drier weather \nwill give way to small rain chances (around 30%) by Saturday, as \nhigh pressure weakens at the surface, and more troughing takes \nplace aloft. Fortunately, dew points will only moderate back into \nthe mid 60s by the start of the next weekend.",
